WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:09.370 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the Africa capitals, episode three of six. First, I clear the
chosen names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10.031 --> 00:00:18.262 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
This episode runs inland from the Gulf of Guinea across the Sahel, and then
out again to the island and the equatorial coast.

3
00:00:18.924 --> 00:00:20.793 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Porto-Novo into view.

4
00:00:26.585 --> 00:00:35.629 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Porto-Novo stands on the lagoon side of Benin, a few kilometres from Cotonou,
the country’s seat of government and largest port.

5
00:00:36.288 --> 00:00:44.427 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was the royal capital of the precolonial Kingdom of Porto-Novo, and the
palace of its king survives beside the lagoon.

6
00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:51.614 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the old royal capital of Dahomey, kept beside the working port of
Cotonou.

7
00:00:52.298 --> 00:00:54.678 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Abuja into view.

8
00:00:59.294 --> 00:01:05.726 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Abuja stands in central Nigeria, inland from the Gulf of Guinea coast, north
of the Niger Delta.

9
00:01:06.382 --> 00:01:17.423 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It became the capital in 1991, chosen because it belonged to no single region;
the Aso Rock rises behind the city and a great mosque dome stands on the
skyline.

10
00:01:18.084 --> 00:01:25.247 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the planned capital on the rock, chosen precisely because it belonged
to nobody’s region.

11
00:01:25.929 --> 00:01:28.564 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Niamey into view.

12
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:39.278 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Niamey stands on the Niger River, in the south-west of Niger, where the
country reaches its river.

13
00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:53.065 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its national museum shows the dinosaur skeletons found in the desert of the
Ténéré, and the city was a village on the river before the French made it the
capital in 1926.

14
00:01:53.718 --> 00:01:59.941 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the capital on the Niger River, west of the gold country and below
the Sahara.

15
00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:03.292 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ouagadougou into view.

16
00:02:07.806 --> 00:02:14.331 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ouagadougou stands in the centre of Burkina Faso, the landlocked country north
of Ghana.

17
00:02:14.992 --> 00:02:27.101 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name means “where people are given honour”, and the city grew as a royal
capital of the Mossi kingdoms; the monument to the heroes still stands beside
the central square.

18
00:02:27.751 --> 00:02:35.251 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the kings’ capital in the middle of Burkina Faso, with the heroes’
statue beside the square.

19
00:02:35.939 --> 00:02:38.667 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Bamako into view.

20
00:02:43.156 --> 00:02:51.817 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bamako stands on the Niger River, in the southwest of Mali, below the Sahara
and above the coastal countries.

21
00:02:52.479 --> 00:03:03.950 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The old city above the river keeps the necropolis of the Askia dynasty, and
the tomb of Askia Mohammed there is a UNESCO World Heritage site.

22
00:03:04.612 --> 00:03:09.941 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the riverside capital with the Askia king’s tomb above the old town.

23
00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.751 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring N'Djamena into view.

24
00:03:17.438 --> 00:03:28.015 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
N'Djamena stands where the Logone flows into the Chari, in the south of Chad,
facing Cameroon across the water, between Libya and the Central African
Republic.

25
00:03:28.672 --> 00:03:39.713 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was called Fort-Lamy until 1973, and its new name is Arabic for “the place
of rest”; it began as a village of fishing weavers.

26
00:03:40.376 --> 00:03:47.969 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the resting place by the Chari, the capital of the tall inland
country below Libya.

27
00:03:48.658 --> 00:03:50.910 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Yaoundé into view.

28
00:03:55.314 --> 00:04:02.814 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yaoundé stands in the forested hills inland from the Gulf of Guinea, in the
south of Cameroon.

29
00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:15.623 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
A spiral monument in the city celebrates the reunification of French and
British Cameroon in 1961, and the national museum holds the country’s forest
and wildlife exhibits.

30
00:04:16.275 --> 00:04:21.604 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the forest capital of Cameroon, with the two-halves monument in the
town.

31
00:04:22.289 --> 00:04:24.797 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Malabo into view.

32
00:04:29.025 --> 00:04:39.091 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Malabo stands on Bioko island in the Gulf of Guinea, in the small country of
Equatorial Guinea, with the mainland strip across the water to the east.

33
00:04:39.745 --> 00:04:50.148 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its bay is a flooded volcanic crater, and behind the town rises Pico Basilé on
an island that sits only a few degrees north of the equator.

34
00:04:50.812 --> 00:04:57.372 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the island capital, across the water from its own mainland strip,
under a volcano.

35
00:04:58.054 --> 00:05:00.306 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Libreville into view.

36
00:05:04.734 --> 00:05:12.025 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Libreville stands on the estuary of the Ogooué River, on the equatorial
Atlantic coast of Gabon.

37
00:05:12.676 --> 00:05:21.465 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was founded in 1849 for Africans freed from a captured slave ship, and its
name is the French word for liberty.

38
00:05:22.115 --> 00:05:28.512 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember “liberty” at the mouth of the Ogooué, on the Atlantic just below the
equator.

39
00:05:34.074 --> 00:05:48.528 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Porto-Novo keeps the old royal capital of Dahomey, Abuja sits on its rock in
the middle of Nigeria, Niamey on the Niger River, Ouagadougou in the middle of
Burkina Faso and Bamako on the river below it.

40
00:05:49.184 --> 00:06:05.780 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the resting place by the Chari at N'Djamena, the forest capital with
the reunification monument at Yaoundé, the island capital under a volcano at
Malabo, and “liberty” at the Ogooué mouth in Libreville.
